                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose and select the idea`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Three pre-screened ideas compete for one runway, and a powerful business-unit sponsor is pressing you to skip validation and “just build” <strong>EntregaFlex</strong> because a retail client “expressed interest”. That verbal maybe is the exact signal that burned <em>R$14M</em> on the last three ventures. Choose on scored criteria — problem severity, evidence, time-to-validation, strategic adjacency and runway fit — not on market size or the loudest voice. <strong>RotaSmart</strong> has the biggest market but a 9–12 month enterprise sales cycle selling to your own competitors; your runway is 9 months. <strong>DevolveJá</strong> attacks an underserved, painful returns problem that fits the core and is cheap to validate. Name the single riskiest assumption you must test first.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Scope the MVP and the validation sprint`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the smallest thing that produces <strong>validated learning</strong>, not the impressive thing the sponsor wants to demo. A disciplined MVP + customer-discovery sprint costs <strong>R$0,9–1,6M</strong>; building a “real” product before validation costs <em>R$4M+ and consumes the runway</em>. Decide what to build versus fake/concierge, which one or two hypotheses to test first, what counts as falsifiable evidence, and how much of the R$9M to spend now versus reserve to pivot. The smartest MVP here is often manual — a concierge service run for ~20 retailers that tests willingness-to-pay next month for a fraction of a full build.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Read the evidence and decide go/no-go/pivot`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The month-3-to-6 results <em>partially contradict</em> the plan: strong interest but willingness-to-pay is half your model, and one customer segment is converting to paid pilots while another is flat. Separate <strong>actionable metrics</strong> (paid-pilot conversion, retention) from <strong>vanity</strong> ones (sign-ups, demo praise). Then make the call against the pre-agreed milestones — <strong>persevere</strong>, <strong>pivot</strong> to the converting segment, or <strong>kill</strong> and return capital — judged on remaining runway and evidence, not on the R$ already spent. Sunk cost is gone either way; the only question is what the runway you have left buys.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover and pitch the gate`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Pitch a three-minute case to the facilitator-played investment committee. Finalise the plan that follows from Round 3 — an R$25M growth ask if go, a re-scoped sprint with revised milestones if pivot, or a clean shutdown that harvests the learning if kill. Show how the runway was spent on <strong>learning, not building</strong>, what the validated metrics prove, and why this cohort breaks the “kill it late” pattern. A real executive will demand you skip the gate and scale now — defend that a timely, evidence-led call beats riding the runway to zero on a verbal maybe.`,

