                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_runway_gone:      `<strong>The runway is essentially gone.</strong> Whatever you learned, there is nothing left to act on it — this is exactly how the last three ventures became expensive late failures. You spent the cash; did it buy learning or just build?`,

                    coach_r1_devolveja:     `<strong>DevolveJá is the runway-aware choice.</strong> A painful, underserved returns problem that fits the core and is cheap to validate — you can reach real evidence inside nine months. Now name the riskiest assumption and test it before you build.`,
                    coach_r1_entregaflex:   `<strong>You funded the sponsor's pick.</strong> EntregaFlex rests on one client who “expressed interest” — a verbal maybe, the same signal that cost Bandeira R$14M. Before you build anything: what is the riskiest assumption, and what cheap test tells you in three weeks whether it's real?`,
                    coach_r1_rotasmart:     `<strong>RotaSmart has a huge TAM — and a 9–12 month enterprise sales cycle selling to your own competitors.</strong> Your runway is 9 months and R$9M. You'll burn the entire runway before closing a single paid pilot. Does the size of the prize matter if you can't reach validation before the money runs out?`,
                    coach_r1_market:        `<strong>Biggest market isn't the same as best venture.</strong> Match the idea to your runway and time-to-validation, not to TAM. A market you can't reach before the money runs out is not a market you can fund.`,
                    coach_r1_sponsor:       `<strong>That's sponsor-driven selection.</strong> The liaison's enthusiasm is not evidence — it's the exact pressure that burned R$14M. Score the ideas on severity, evidence, time-to-validation and runway fit, then defend the choice to the sponsor.`,
                    coach_r1_noassumption:  `<strong>“The demand is obvious” is how you build before validating.</strong> Every venture has a riskiest assumption — usually whether customers will pay. Name it and test it first, or you'll learn the answer the expensive way.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp selection.</strong> You chose the runway-reachable idea on scored criteria and named willingness-to-pay as the riskiest assumption. That is exactly the discipline the new gate demands — evidence over enthusiasm.`,

                    coach_r2_concierge:     `<strong>A concierge MVP maximises learning per R$.</strong> Running it manually for ~20 retailers tests willingness-to-pay next month for a fraction of a full build — and keeps the runway for the iteration you'll need.`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>A landing-page gauges interest cheaply</strong> — useful, but interest isn't willingness-to-pay. Pair it with a concierge test that puts a real price in front of real customers, or you'll mistake clicks for demand.`,
                    coach_r2_fullbuild:     `<strong>Your “MVP” is a fully built platform costing R$4,2M and four months.</strong> You've spent half your runway and learned nothing yet — the exact mistake that burned R$14M. What could you fake — a concierge service for 20 retailers — that tests willingness-to-pay for R$300k instead?`,
                    coach_r2_signups:       `<strong>Sign-ups and a thrilled demo are vanity metrics.</strong> They flatter a failing idea. Your milestone is 35% paid-pilot conversion — define falsifiable evidence around money committed, not applause.`,
                    coach_r2_opinion:       `<strong>More verbal interest is not evidence.</strong> You're stacking opinions on top of the opinion that got you here. Test willingness-to-pay with a real price and a real commitment, not another conversation.`,
                    coach_r2_underspend:    `<strong>That sprint is too thin to learn anything.</strong> Under-funding discovery means you reach the month-6 gate with a guess instead of a decision. A disciplined sprint is R$0,9–1,6M — spend enough to get falsifiable evidence.`,
                    coach_r2_overspend:     `<strong>You're over-spending the validation sprint.</strong> Discovery doesn't need this much — the rest is runway you'll want to pivot with. Keep the sprint disciplined and reserve cash for the iteration after the evidence arrives.`,
                    coach_r2_broad:         `<strong>Testing everything at once tests nothing well.</strong> Scatter the sprint and no single result is clean enough to decide on. Test the one riskiest assumption first with falsifiable criteria.`,

                    coach_r3_actionable:    `<strong>That's reading the evidence properly.</strong> One segment converting to paid pilots while another is flat is the actionable signal — the blended average was hiding it. Now decide on that signal and the runway you have left.`,
                    coach_r3_vanity:        `<strong>1,800 sign-ups and a thrilled demo — lovely.</strong> How many committed a paid pilot? Your milestone was 35% conversion. Sign-ups and applause are vanity; paid-pilot conversion and retention are the metrics that decide go/no-go.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring contradicting evidence is how ventures fail late.</strong> The data is telling you something the plan didn't predict. The discipline is to listen to it, not to defend the plan because it's the plan.`,
                    coach_r3_pivot:         `<strong>A pivot while runway remains is the textbook move.</strong> You're keeping the vision and changing the path — refocusing on the segment that is actually converting, with cash still in the bank to make it count. That is lean discipline, not failure.`,
                    coach_r3_killgood:      `<strong>A timely no-go is a success of the gate, not a personal failure.</strong> The milestones are missed and traction is thin — killing now returns capital and credibility and keeps Bandeira Labs alive for the next idea. That breaks the “kill it late” pattern.`,
                    coach_r3_killearly:     `<strong>Careful — you're killing a venture that's actually converting.</strong> Traction has cleared a meaningful bar; a kill here throws away real validated learning. A pivot or a disciplined persevere would put the remaining runway to better use.`,
                    coach_r3_persevere_ok:  `<strong>Persevering is defensible here.</strong> Traction has cleared the bar, so scaling toward the R$25M round is backed by evidence rather than hope. Keep measuring the actionable metrics so you'd still see a turn if it came.`,
                    coach_r3_perseverebad:  `<strong>You want to push on because you've already spent the money.</strong> That R$ is gone either way — the only question is what the remaining runway buys: a pivot to the segment that's converting, or three more months proving the same negative. Which is the better use of the runway you have left?`,
                    coach_r3_sunk:          `<strong>You're letting sunk cost drive the call.</strong> The money already spent is irrelevant to the decision — what matters is what the remaining runway and the evidence say. Decide forward, not backward.`,
                    coach_r3_noreserve:     `<strong>No ring-fenced runway means no room to act.</strong> Whatever you decide, you need cash left to do it — a pivot or a scale both cost money. Reserve enough runway to make the next move real.`,

                    coach_r4_evidenceled:   `<strong>An evidence-led plan is what the gate is for.</strong> You're matching the ask to what the validated metrics actually prove — a R$25M ask only if the evidence earns it, a clean harvest if not. That is corporate-venturing governance done right.`,
                    coach_r4_scaleanyway:   `<strong>“Momentum will sort the metrics out” is the R$14M mistake.</strong> Asking for the full round regardless of the evidence is exactly how the last three ventures scaled before they were validated. The gate exists to stop precisely this.`,
                    coach_r4_threemonths:   `<strong>“Just three more months” is how late failures are born.</strong> That's what the last three ventures said. Riding the runway to prove the same negative again burns the cash and the credibility. A disciplined call now beats a hopeful extension.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchlearning: `<strong>That's a board-ready pitch.</strong> You showed how the runway bought learning — paid-pilot conversion, unit economics, time-to-MVP — and matched the ask to the evidence. That is exactly how this cohort breaks the “kill it late” pattern.`,
                    coach_r4_demo:          `<strong>A polished demo and a sign-up count isn't validation.</strong> The committee has seen impressive demos die before — what they need is the paid-pilot conversion and the unit-economics path. Lead with learning, not applause.`,
                    coach_r4_sponsorword:   `<strong>You're pitching the sponsor's enthusiasm again.</strong> Verbal interest from one client is exactly the signal that burned R$14M. Put validated metrics in front of the committee, not the optimism that got you here.`,
                    coach_r4_cave:          `<strong>Caving to the executive abandons the gate.</strong> Skipping validation because the sponsor outranks you is precisely the pattern the new rule was built to break. A real Corporate-Venture Lead defends the gate with a cheap test, not a salute.`,

